Masume — attribution notice
===========================

This repository is public, which means the data in it is being distributed. The
obligations below follow from that, and they apply to the datasets rather than to
the application code.


Stroke order data — src/data/kvg-strokes.json
---------------------------------------------

Extracted from KanjiVG (https://kanjivg.tagaini.net/), by Ulrich Apel and
contributors, licensed under Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0.

Only the characters this app draws were extracted (the 150 kana set plus the 79
N5 kanji, 229 in total), and only the stroke path data; the paths themselves are
unmodified.

KanjiVG was chosen over @k1low/hanzi-writer-data-jp because the latter derives from
Chinese font outlines and disagrees with Japanese handwriting convention on 21 of
those 150 characters. See docs/ARCHITECTURE.md.

The superseded licence texts for that package remain in
licenses/hanzi-writer-data-jp/ for reference; no data from it ships any more.


Typefaces — public/fonts/*.woff2
--------------------------------

Three families ship with the app, subset by scripts/subset-fonts.mjs to the ~290
glyphs it can display. All three are under the SIL Open Font License 1.1, and the
full licence text for each is in licenses/:

  - Zen Kaku Gothic New — © The Zen Kaku Gothic Project Authors
    (licenses/zen-kaku-gothic/OFL.txt)
  - Zen Old Mincho — © The Zen Old Mincho Project Authors
    (licenses/zen-old-mincho/OFL.txt)
  - IBM Plex Mono — © IBM Corp., Reserved Font Name "Plex"
    (licenses/ibm-plex/OFL.txt)

Serving these from Google Fonts made Google the distributor. Self-hosting moves
that role here, and the OFL requires the licence to travel with the files — which
is why the texts above are in the repository and named on the /tentang/ screen
rather than merely linked.

The files are subsets: glyphs outside the set were removed, and nothing else about
the fonts was modified. Neither family is renamed, so the Reserved Font Name terms
are untouched.


Kana dataset — src/data/kana.json
---------------------------------

Compiled for this project from the standard gojūon structure, and generated
deterministically by scripts/generate-kana.mjs. No third-party dataset was copied.


Vocabulary, kanji and grammar datasets — src/data/{vocab,kanji,grammar}_n5.json
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Built by scripts/fetch-jlpt.mjs from OpenJLPT (https://github.com/evanclan/OpenJLPT),
Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 (licenses/openjlpt/). OpenJLPT itself
draws on:

  - JMdict / EDICT (Electronic Dictionary Research and Development Group),
    Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0
  - KANJIDIC2 (EDRDG), Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0
  - Jonathan Waller's JLPT level tables (tanos.co.uk),
    Creative Commons Attribution 3.0
  - Tatoeba example sentences, Creative Commons Attribution 2.0 France

Part-of-speech tags (and the verb group derived from them) come from
jmdict-simplified (https://github.com/scriptin/jmdict-simplified), a JSON
edition of the same JMdict data listed above, under the same CC BY-SA licence.

What was changed: entries were reshaped into the app's generic item form, and at
most two example sentences were kept per item. The derived files remain under
CC BY-SA 4.0.

Grammar points not present in OpenJLPT (29 of 49) are written for this project and
marked "source": "compiled" in the data, as opposed to "source": "openjlpt". The
same marking covers the vocabulary supplement (greetings, 日本語, and 私 read as
わたし rather than the humble わたくし JMdict lists first) — the Waller lists file
greetings separately, so OpenJLPT never carried them. None of it is copied from
Bunpro, JLPT Sensei, Minna no Nihongo, Irodori, or any other course material.


Unit curriculum — scripts/data/units-n5.json
--------------------------------------------

Written for this project. The ORDER in which grammar is introduced follows the
sequence used by Minna no Nihongo, which is a fact about a widely-published
syllabus rather than a copyrightable work — the same sequence third parties
publish openly and other apps offer as a study path. No sentence, dialogue,
illustration or explanation from that book, from Irodori, or from any other
course is reproduced here: every dialogue and example sentence in this file was
written for Masume, and machine-checked by scripts/verify-units.mjs to use only
material the learner has already been taught.

Each unit carries "reviewed": false until a native speaker or teacher has read
it. Until then the app says so on screen — see docs/PETA-MATERI.md.


